#!/usr/bin/python3
"""hearth-network: Hearth Settings' VPN, Mobile hotspot, Proxy and connection details pages (Network & internet), as
you (no root). Everything about a connection is NetworkManager's (nmcli, always an argument list, never a shell): Hearth
keeps no copy of it. Secrets (a VPN's keys and passwords, the hotspot's password) stay in NetworkManager: they go to
nmcli on its stdin (never on a command line, never in a Hearth file or log), and NetworkManager keeps them its own way.
The proxy is Hearth's own setting (~/.config/nimbus/proxy.json, written through hearth_settings_io), handed on to GTK
apps (gsettings), Qt/KDE apps (kioslaverc) and programs that read http_proxy (~/.config/environment.d).

  hearth-network status              JSON: every saved connection {uuid, name, type, kind, device, active,
                                     autoconnect}, VPNs, the Wi-Fi device, whether it can be a hotspot, the plugins
  hearth-network vpn-import FILE     a WireGuard (.conf) or OpenVPN (.ovpn) file into NetworkManager; JSON {uuid, name}
  hearth-network vpn-up UUID         connect; stdin (optional) {"user": "...", "password": "..."} for OpenVPN
                                     accounts: handed to nmcli's passwd-file on its stdin, used for this connection
  hearth-network vpn-down UUID | vpn-remove UUID
  hearth-network details UUID        JSON: a connection's DNS, IP settings, metered, MAC address, auto-connect and
                                     what it has now (address, gateway, DNS)
  hearth-network details-set UUID    stdin {"dns": ["1.1.1.1"], "autoDns": true, "metered": "yes|no|unknown",
                                     "mac": "permanent|preserve|random|stable|default", "autoconnect": true,
                                     "ipv4": {"method": "auto|manual", "address": "192.168.1.5/24", "gateway": "..."},
                                     "ipv6": "auto|disabled|ignore"}. Only the keys sent change; then reapplied
  hearth-network hotspot             JSON: the hotspot {on, ssid, band, uuid, device, canHotspot, clients}
  hearth-network hotspot-on          stdin {"ssid": "...", "password": "...", "band": "bg|a|"}: make or update
                                     "Hearth Hotspot" and start it (the password set through nmcli's editor on stdin)
  hearth-network hotspot-off
  hearth-network hotspot-password    JSON {password}: the hotspot's password, read from NetworkManager when you ask
  hearth-network proxy               JSON: the proxy settings
  hearth-network proxy-set           stdin {"mode": "none|auto|manual", "url": "...", "http": "host:port",
                                     "https": "host:port", "socks": "host:port", "ignore": "localhost, 127.0.0.1"}
  hearth-network proxy-apply         hand the saved proxy on to apps again (each sign-in; writes nothing of Hearth's)
1.14, the rest of KDE's network settings (docs/SYSTEM-SETTINGS.md, "Network"):
  hearth-network vpn-add             stdin {"type": "l2tp|pptp|openconnect|sstp|fortisslvpn|ipsec", "name", "gateway",
                                     "user", "password", "psk", "group", "domain", "realm", "protocol"}: a VPN typed in
                                     by hand. Only types whose NetworkManager plugin is installed (status: vpnTypes)
  hearth-network enterprise-set UUID stdin {"eap": "peap|ttls|tls|pwd", "identity", "anonymous", "caCert", "phase2",
                                     "domain", "clientCert", "privateKey", "password", "keyPassword"} (802.1X, for
                                     Wi-Fi and wired); {"off": true} takes 802.1X off a wired connection
  hearth-network wifi-enterprise-add stdin {"ssid", ...the same}: an enterprise (WPA-EAP) Wi-Fi network
  hearth-network wired-add           stdin {"name", "device"}: a wired connection of your own (a second profile)
  hearth-network mobile-add          stdin {"name", "apn", "user", "password", "pin", "roaming"}: mobile broadband
  hearth-network mobile-set UUID     stdin {"apn", "user", "password", "pin", "roaming"} (only the keys sent change)
  hearth-network wifi-qr UUID        JSON {ssid, security, password, size, rows}: a Wi-Fi network's QR code (rows of
                                     "0"/"1", made by qrencode on its stdin); read only when you ask for it
  hearth-network duplicate UUID      a copy of a connection ("Name (copy)"); JSON {uuid, name}
Passwords (802.1X, mobile broadband, a VPN's) go to NetworkManager through `nmcli connection edit` on its stdin and
NetworkManager keeps them (its own secret store): never on a command line, in a Hearth file or a log.
Output: one JSON line; failures print "error: ..." and exit 1.
"""
